Findings:

- **Toolchain changes.** Embedded version strings show that every large Rust pair is built by
  a different rustc (`fd`: 1.80.1 → 1.89.0; ripgrep linux: 1.76 → 1.77 and at the same time
  non-PIE → static-PIE; `bat` and ripgrep windows: different rustc commits), and `lazygit`
  0.40.2 → 0.44.0 goes from go1.20.7 to go1.22.6. In those pairs 25–59% of `.text` is not
  matched to anything and 6–11% of the matched part differs: the compiler regenerated the code.
  That is first-order change in the thesis's terms and no address model recovers it. In the
  Rust pairs the new code is where the bytes go: `.text` is 79% of the stream for ripgrep
  windows and 52% for bat, read-only constants most of the rest. By contrast `fzf` (go1.20.13
  on both sides) and `lazygit` 0.43.1 → 0.44.0 (go1.22.5 → go1.22.6) patch at 2% and 5.5%.
- **Tables that mirror the code are a second cost in those pairs**: `.pdata` (99.9% unmatched
  in ripgrep windows, 59 kB of stream), `__unwind_info` (99.8% in bat), `__gcc_except_tab` (71%),
  `.eh_frame_hdr`, `.typelink` (95% in both Go pairs), the Mach-O symbol table in `__LINKEDIT`
  (77–86%). Several are sorted arrays of addresses or offsets in which one insertion shifts
  every later entry, so no exact match of useful length survives and bsdiff 4.3 gives up on
  them. They are 0.1–3% of the file but cost 2–7% of the stream, and the address-like ones
  would be nearly free with the hypotheses of this section *if they were aligned*: an
  alignment-side fix (a mismatch-tolerant aligner carries an offset through them; bsdiff 4.3's
  exact seeds do not).
- **In Go binaries the metadata costs more than the code.** In the same-toolchain pair, `.text`
  is 44% of the file and 15% of the stream (56 kB); `.gopclntab` is 32% of the file and **49% of
  the stream** (184 kB), `.rodata` another 33%. Even across Go 1.20 → 1.22, `__gopclntab` costs
  1.31 MB against 1.15 MB for `__text`.
- **Part of that is predictable structure.** In the same-toolchain pair `.gopclntab` has
  495,295 differing matched bytes of which the generic hypotheses explain 7%, and `.rodata`
  389,737 with 47%. Many of those fields are 32-bit offsets relative to a table base that
  itself moves: function entry offsets relative to `.text`, name offsets into `funcnametab`,
  `pcsp`/`pcfile`/`pcln` offsets into `pctab`, type and name offsets relative to the type
  section. A **base-relative offset32 hypothesis** (`v' = FWD[base + v] − base'`) raises the
  explained share of all differing bytes in this pair from 40% to **51%** with the bases taken
  from the section table alone, and to **64.5%** with the three `pclntab` sub-tables read from
  Go's `pcHeader` (a Go-aware parser on the diff side only; the patch would carry (old base,
  new base) pairs). **Coded size** (verified), bytes for matched data per section:

{{Tgo}}

  `.rodata` drops 17% from the section bases alone (type and name offsets are relative to the
  start of `.rodata`); `.gopclntab` drops 11% and only with the Go-aware bases. The oracle's
  11–24 points become 8–12% of matched-data bytes (5–7% of this patch) because the coder was
  already predicting part of these fields from their neighbours, and because most of what is
  left in `pclntab` is not offsets but the pc-value tables themselves (delta-coded varint
  streams that change whenever the function's code does).

So: the 25–35% patches are genuinely new code plus tables that mirror it, caused by toolchain
changes. Go's metadata is the largest single cost in Go binaries and is partly predictable
structure, worth about a tenth of the matched-data bytes on a same-toolchain upgrade.

