## 5. The difference map and apply-side resources

### 5.1 Is there a streaming alternative to BWT + midpoint that matches it in size?

Yes, and it is much smaller. On the format agent's own inputs (the 14 pairs up to 3.7 MB; its
greedy aligner ported to C and checked to reproduce its section totals exactly; little-endian
mode), bytes:

{{T5}}

- **Map: 175,041 → 72,166 bytes (−59%)** with the conditional flag model alone, **60,843
  (−65%)** with address hypotheses. Real coder, decoded and compared. The format agent's best
  direct model (old byte + 8 previous map bits) was 193,407.
- **Non-zero string: 475,696 → 331,401 (−30%)**, 298,084 (−37%) with address hypotheses.
- Where it comes from (ablations, section 9.2): removing the old bytes *after* the position
  from the contexts raises the flag cost by 27% (72,166 → 91,788), which is still half of the
  BWT coder's; removing the old-window memories costs 2% on flags and 3% on values. Most of the
  gain is simply conditioning each map bit on the old bytes around it and on the recent digits,
  with mixing.

The BWT clusters map positions that have similar *map* context; it cannot use the old file.
But the map is not self-contained structure: whether a byte changes depends on whether it is
part of an address field, and that is visible in the old bytes around it.

### 5.2 Apply-side resources

| | bsdiff 4.3 | thesis format (as implemented) | conditional stream |
|---|---|---|---|
| order of output | streaming, new-file order | whole map decoded first; big-endian mode walks each record backwards | streaming, new-file order |
| memory beyond the old file and the output | three bzip2 decoders (about 4 MB each) | D/8 + D/16 bytes working memory for the inverse BWT, the map itself (D bytes, or D/8 bit-packed), the decompressed non-zero string | model tables (fixed: 4–64 MB by configuration, section 3.4); for address hypotheses the segment list (12 bytes per control record) with binary search, or 4 bytes per old byte as a flat array |
| random access needed | old file | old file | old file |
| CPU | fast | fast (inverse BWT + LZ decode) | symmetric: equal to the encoder's coding cost |
| encode-side extras | — | one suffix sort of the D-symbol map per candidate mode (four) | none |

With the corrected inverse-BWT memory (D/8 + D/16 bytes), memory is no argument against the
BWT map. What remains: it is not streaming, it costs four suffix sorts at encode time, and it
is 2.4–2.9× larger than the conditional alternative.

**A patch format that applies in O(1) memory beyond the old file** is the conditional stream
with the control records front-loaded (they are small), the extra string as its own LZ stream
with a bounded window (Brotli at most 16 MiB), and the address map evaluated by binary search
over the segment list. It costs nothing in ratio: it is the best-compressing variant measured
here. The price is apply CPU (section 3.4).

### 5.3 A drop-in middle step

The flag model alone can replace the BWT + midpoint coder as `mapModel = 1` in the existing
layout: the non-zero string is stored before the map, so the map decoder can consume digits as
it decodes flags and use them as context. That keeps the non-zero string in zlib/Brotli and
takes the −59% on the map only: on the 14-pair set, map + non-zero would go from 650,737 to
about 548,000 bytes (−16%; a sum of two measured columns) instead of 403,567 (−38%).

## 6. Diff-side resources

### 6.1 Alternatives for the alignment stage (analysis)

n = old size, m = new size. Memory is index memory besides the two files.

| method | index memory | finds | remark |
|---|---|---|---|
| SA-IS over old‖new + LCP (DESIGN §5) | 4(n+m) + 4(n+m) + 8m result: about 9 bytes per input byte, 13 at peak with a third int array | the longest match at every new position, exactly | the plan |
| suffix array of **old only** + matching statistics (binary search per new position, as bsdiff 4.3 does at its scan positions, or the linear-time walk with LCP/ISA) | 4n (+4n) + 8m result | exactly the same information | halves the index memory when n ≈ m; plain binary search needs care on repetitive data |
| sampled suffix array (every k-th suffix of old) | 4n/k | every match longer than about 2k, at nearly full length | needs a sparse suffix sorter that is safe on repetitive inputs; the DP only profits from matches long enough to pay for a 20-point switch, so k = 4–8 is plausible; not prototyped |
| FM-index of old, backward-search matching statistics | about 1–2 n | exact | 5–10× slower; wavelet tree + rank/select is a large port |
| hash of k-byte seeds (xdelta3, zstd LDM, HDiffPatch `-s`, bidiff) | 4n/step | exact seeds of length ≥ k, few candidates each | fastest; cost measured in 6.2 |
| minimizers + chaining (minimap2) | about 2.4 bytes per base in minimap2 at k=15, w=10 (literature) | diagonals with exact runs ≥ w+k−1 | the chaining step *is* what the pruned DP does; sampling the seeds would only save index memory |
| FFT block alignment (thesis ch. 1) | O(√(n log n)) | the best offset per ≈ √(n ln n)-byte block under any mismatch pattern | its unique property is the memory bound |
| spaced seeds, e.g. care pattern (1100)⁴ | 4n per pattern | regions whose matching bytes follow the pattern (address tables with stable high bytes) | cheap, but O(n) memory and blind to irregular mismatch patterns |

